# The obligation

`EigenvalueShiftBound.SingleCouplingFormPreservation afr` is the honest residual of Clay
core input (1): the real 4D SU(2) block-spin RG step must map a single-coupling
(heat-kernel/Wilson) action to an effective action that is STILL controlled by one
renormalized coupling — i.e. the INDUCED couplings (multi-plaquette / higher-rep /
non-abelian commutator terms) generated by integrating out the fine modes are
bounded/negligible, so no separate additive back-reaction survives in the
multiplicity-blind eigenvalue.  This is the classic constructive-YM difficulty.

# The induced coupling, made CONCRETE (this module)

Under one block-spin step the effective action develops connected (Ursell) contributions
from the fine-mode fluctuations.  The FIRST genuinely interacting one — two plaquettes
sharing a link, `MayerTwoPlaquette.two_plaquette_identity` — has connected activity
`ζ₂(w,r) = (coeffW w r)²` on a common excited rep `r ≥ 1`
(`MayerTwoPlaquette.connectedActivity2_eqv`).  Its LEADING term is

    inducedCoupling w  :=  (coeffW w 1)²  =  (2·w³)²  =  4·w⁶,

the induced 2-plaquette coupling, versus the single-plaquette gap-channel term
`coeffW w 1 = 2·w³`.  The RATIO is EXACTLY the polymer ACTIVITY

    inducedCoupling w / (coeffW w 1)  =  coeffW w 1  =  2·w³.

So the induced coupling is suppressed relative to the single-coupling term by exactly the
connected-cluster activity `2·w³` — SMALL only when `2·w³ < 1`, i.e. at STRONG coupling
`w < 2^{-1/3} ≈ 0.7937` (`input1_numerics`/`cluster.py`: ratio `2·w³` verified exact).

# What is PROVED here (axiom-clean `Q'`/`Nat`)

  * `inducedCoupling_le_single` — form-preservation core: at strong coupling `0 ≤ w ≤ 1/2`
    the induced 2-plaquette coupling is DOMINATED by the single-coupling term,
    `inducedCoupling w ≤ coeffW w 1`.  (Sharper: `inducedCoupling_le_quarter_single`,
    factor `≤ 1/4`.)  Via the proved `coeffW1_le_quarter` (`2·w³ ≤ 1/4` on `w ≤ 1/2`).
  * `inducedCoupling_le_afDefect` — the REDUCTION: under the cluster-activity gate
    `ClusterActivityBound` (the induced activity is bounded by the SUMMABLE model defect
    `afDefectSeq afr`) and strong coupling, the induced coupling per block step is bounded
    by a summable sequence — `Σ inducedCoupling < ∞`, the cluster expansion converges,
    form is preserved.  This is form-preservation reduced to the sharp cluster gate.
  * `formPreservation_of_afStep` — a guard-safe conditional producer of
    `SingleCouplingFormPreservation` (open binder on the AF β-step + large-field data;
    the structure is the free-field reabsorbed form, as its own docstring flags).
  * `cluster_gate_excludes_weak` — the ADVERSARIAL crux (Rule 6): the cluster gate is
    PROVABLY UNSATISFIABLE at weak coupling.  Because `afDefectSeq ≤ 1` and the gate forces
    `coeffW (w_k) 1 ≤ afDefectSeq afr k ≤ 1`, ANY scale with `coeffW (w_k) 1 > 1` refutes
    the gate — and `coeffW_weak_gt_one` proves `coeffW (9/10) 1 = 1.458 > 1`.  So the
    character/cluster expansion does NOT converge at weak coupling `w → 1` (the
    continuum-relevant deep-UV regime): form-preservation via THIS expansion holds only at
    strong coupling and PROVABLY fails to be gated near the free fixed point.

# HONEST VERDICT (adversarial, AGENTS.md Rule 6/9)

Form-preservation is GENUINELY PROVABLE at strong coupling: the induced (multi-plaquette,
higher-rep) couplings are the connected Ursell contributions, suppressed by the polymer
